==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|litter bug|litter-bug}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|litter|bug}}. Coined in 1947 by New York copywriter Paul B. Gioni for The American Ad Council,<ref>{{cite-web|title=Pollution: Keep America Beautiful - Iron Eyes Cody (1961–1983)|work=w:Ad Council|accessdate=10 July 2018|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20081030084003/http://www.adcouncil.org/default.aspx?id=132|archivedate=30 October 2008}}</ref> perhaps punning on {{m|en|jitterbug}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlɪtɚˌbʌɡ/|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} A person who tends to drop [[litter]] and not clean it up.
#: {{syn|en|litter lout}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1967|author=w:Arlo Guthrie|title=w:Alice's Restaurant|passage={{...}} I mean I'm sittin' here, on the Group W bench, 'cause you want to know if I'm moral enough to join the army, burn women, kids, houses and villages, after being a '''litterbug'''}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Nicole Hemmer|title=The striking parallels between the assaults on Charlottesville and the Capitol|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/01/08/opinions/capitol-violence-charlottesville-antifa-myth-hemmer|date=2021-01-08|passage=Pretending a '''litterbug''' and a spree killer have committed equivalent infractions doesn’t make people safer.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-US|author=Sam Roberts|title=John J. Doherty, Stalwart Sanitation Commissioner, Dies at 84|work=w:The New York Times|date=2023-04-18|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/18/nyregion/john-j-doherty-dead.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Mr. Doherty was known to publicly berate '''litterbugs''' and upbraid motorists whose stranded cars prevented plows from clearing snow.|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/Im93H|archivedate=2024-12-26}}

